[' Below is a high-level structure of the project in Rust using `tokio` for asynchronous operations, `reqwest` for HTTP requests, `serde` for JSON serialization/deserialization, `confy` for configuration management, and `tracing` for logging. The code provided does not include all the features requested but serves as a starting point.

```rust
// Cargo.toml
[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["json", "blocking"] }
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
serde_json = "1"
confy = "0.5"
tracing = "0.1"
tracing-subscriber = "0.3"
tokio-native-tls = "0.4"

// src/main.rs
use confy::{load, Confy};
use reqwest::Client;
use serde_json::{Map, Value};
use std::collections::HashSet;
use tracing::*;
use tokio::runtime::Runtime;
use tokio::sync::RwLock;

mod service {
    pub trait Service {
        async fn new(config: &Map<String, Value>) -> Self;
        async fn health_check(&self) -> bool;
        async fn execute(&self, request: &Map<String, Value>) -> Result<Value, reqwest::Error>;
    }
}

struct ClientManager {
    services: RwLock<HashSet<Box<dyn service::Service + Send + Sync>>>,
    client: Client,
}

impl ClientManager {
    async fn new(config: &Map<String, Value>) -> Self {
        let mut services = HashSet::new();
        for (name, service_config) in config.get("services").unwrap().members() {
            let service = service::Service::new(service_config).await;
            if service.health_check().await {
                services.insert(Box::new(service));
            }
        }

        ClientManager {
            services: RwLock::new(services),
            client: Client::builder()
                .connect_timeout(Duration::from_secs(10))
                .build(),
        }
    }

    // Other methods for executing requests and managing retries, backoffs, etc.
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let conf = load().expect("Failed to load configuration");
    let client_manager = ClientManager::new(&conf).await;

    // Use the client manager for making requests and handling retries, backoffs, etc.
}
```

This is a starting point for building your HTTP client in Rust. The code provided does not include all the features requested, but serves as a foundation that you can build upon. For handling certificates, consider using `native-tls` along with `reqwest`. To implement retry mechanisms, you could use `tokio::task::retry` or write your own backoff strategy.

For testing, create test cases for each service implementation and test the integration of services with the client manager. Document the configuration options and usage patterns clearly to make it easier for others to understand and contribute to your project.

For optimizing performance under high concurrency, consider using `rayon` or similar libraries for parallel tasks and data structures, as well as monitoring request success rates and error rates using tools like `prometheus`.']

