[' I'll provide you a high-level architecture for this distributed system using Microservices design pattern, focusing on the User Profile Service. This service will leverage Redis for in-memory caching and PostgreSQL as the remote relational database. It will also integrate with a third-party authentication service (e.g., OAuth2 provider) and use Circuit Breaker pattern to handle potential service disruptions.

Here's the structure of the User Profile Service:

1. **UserProfileService** - Main microservice, containing API endpoints for fetching user profiles (GET /api/v1/users/{id}) and updating them (PUT /api/v1/users/{id}). This service will implement retry logic with exponential backoff using Hystrix or similar library.

2. **CacheService** - Responsible for managing Redis cache, using the Cache-Aside pattern to load user data only when necessary and update the cache on subsequent requests.

3. **AuthService** - Integrates with a third-party authentication service (e.g., OAuth2 provider) to verify user credentials before accessing their data.

4. **DatabaseClient** - Handles communication with PostgreSQL, providing methods for fetching and updating user data from the database.

5. **ExceptionHandler** - Centralized exception handling class, handling exceptions thrown during service execution and logging errors.

6. **CircuitBreakerDecorator** - Decorates API endpoints in UserProfileService and AuthService to implement Circuit Breaker pattern.

7. **UnitTests** - Contains test cases for verifying correctness of caching logic, authentication integration, and exception handling.

8. **Dockerfile** - For containerizing the microservice using Docker and Docker Compose.

Here's a code snippet demonstrating the Cache-Aside pattern implementation in UserProfileService:

```java
@Component
public class UserProfileService implements IUserProfileService {

    @Autowired
    private RedisCache redisCache;

    @Autowired
    private DatabaseClient databaseClient;

    //...

    public User getUserById(Long id) {
        String cacheKey = "user:" + id;
        User userInCache = redisCache.get(cacheKey);

        if (userInCache != null) return userInCache;

        User dbUser = databaseClient.fetchUserById(id);
        redisCache.set(cacheKey, dbUser);
        return dbUser;
    }

    public void updateUser(Long id, User user) {
        //...

        databaseClient.updateUser(id, user);
        String cacheKey = "user:" + id;
        redisCache.delete(cacheKey);
    }
}
```

Note that this is a high-level design and doesn't cover all the implementation details or error handling cases. You should consult best practices for each technology used (e.g., Redis, PostgreSQL, Hystrix, etc.) to ensure robustness and performance under heavy load.

Lastly, don't forget to document your assumptions, trade-offs, and reasons for choosing specific technologies during implementation. This will help others understand the design choices made and make it easier to maintain and evolve the system in the future.']

